"War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/34986)\nciteturn46search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; # War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics ... Oxford University Press ... Jeffrey A.Friedman ... 17 April 2019\n\n# War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.001.0001\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780190938055\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780190938024\n\nPublisher:\n\nOxford University Press\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\n[Button: Disclosure]\n\nAs an Amazon Associate OUP earns from qualifying purchases.\n\nBook\n\n# War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nAssistant Professor of Political Science, Dartmouth College\n\nFind on\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nPublished:\n\n17 April 2019\n\nAvailable in print:\n\n4 July 2019\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.001.0001\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780190938055\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780190938024\n\nPublisher:\n\nOxford University Press\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\n[Button: Disclosure]\n\nAs an Amazon Associate OUP earns from qualifying purchases.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWar and Chance analyzes the logic, psychology, and politics of assessing uncertainty in international affairs. It explains how the most important kinds of uncertainty in international politics are inherently subjective, and yet how scholars, practitioners, and pundits can still debate these issues in clear and structured ways. Altogether, the book shows how foreign policy analysts can assess uncertainty in a manner that is theoretically coherent, empirically meaningful, politically defensible, practically useful, and sometimes logically necessary for making sound choices. Each of these claims contradicts widespread skepticism about the value of probabilistic reasoning in international politics, and shows how placing greater emphasis on assessing uncertainty can improve nearly any kind of foreign policy analysis or decision. The book substantiates this argument by examining critical episodes in the history of U.S. national security policy, such as strategic planning in Vietnam, assessments of Iraq’s weapons of mass destruction programs, and the search for Osama bin Laden. The book also draws on a diverse range of quantitative evidence, including a database containing nearly one million geopolitical forecasts and experimental studies involving hundreds of national security professionals.\n\nKeywords: international relations, national security, decision making, uncertainty, risk assessment, probability, forecasting, intelligence analysis, political psychology, survey experiments\n\nContents\n\n  * ### Front Matter\n\n    * Copyright Page\n    * Dedication\n    * Acknowledgments\n  * Expand Introduction: “One of the Things You Learn as President Is That You’re Always Dealing with Probabilities”\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 1 Pathologies of Probability Assessment\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 2 Subjective Probability and International Politics\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 3 The Value of Precision in Probability Assessment\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 4 Dispelling Illusions of Rigor\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 5 The Politics of Uncertainty and Blame\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 6 Analysis and Decision\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 7 Placing Probability Front and Center in Foreign Policy Discourse\n\nView chapter\n\n  * ### End Matter\n\n    * Expand Appendix Supporting Information for Chapters 3–5\n    * Index--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroduction: “One of the Things You Learn as President Is That You’re Always Dealing with Probabilities” | War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/34986/chapter-abstract/298660617)\nciteturn46search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International PoliticsJeffrey A. ... Friedman, Oxford University Press (2019).\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.001.0001\n\nPublished:\n\n17 April 2019\n\nAvailable in print:\n\n04 July 2019\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780190938055\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780190938024\n\nChapter\n\n# Introduction: “One of the Things You Learn as President Is That You’re Always Dealing with Probabilities”\n\nGet access\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nFind on\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.003.0001\n\nPages\n\n1–16\n\n  * Published:\n\n17 April 2019\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis chapter describes widespread skepticism regarding the value of assessing uncertainty in international politics. “Agnostics” argue that assessments of uncertainty in international politics are too unreliable to be useful for shaping major foreign policy decisions. “Rejectionists” argue that attempting to assess uncertainty in international politics can be counterproductive, surrounding foreign policy analyses with illusions of rigor or exposing foreign policy analysts to excessive criticism. “Cynics” claim that foreign policy analysts and decision makers have self-interested motives to avoid assessing uncertainty. The chapter explains how these ideas lead many scholars, practitioners, and pundits to avoid holding careful debates about the risks surrounding major foreign policy choices. The chapter describes how this aversion to probabilistic reasoning appears in several high-profile cases, such as President Kennedy’s decision to authorize the Bay of Pigs invasion and President Obama’s decision to raid Osama bin Laden’s compound in Abbottabad, Pakistan.\n\nKeywords: international relations, national security, decision making, uncertainty, risk, risk assessment, risk analysis, probability, forecasting, intelligence analysis\n\nSubject\n\nInternational Relations\n\nCollection: Oxford Scholarship Online\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics. Jeffrey A. Friedman, Oxford University Press (2019). © Jeffrey A. Friedman. DOI: 10.1093/oso/9780190938024.003.0001\n\nYou do not currently have access to this chapter.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPathologies of Probability Assessment | War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/34986/chapter-abstract/298660735)\nciteturn46search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International PoliticsJeffrey A. ... Friedman, Oxford University Press (2019).\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.001.0001\n\nPublished:\n\n17 April 2019\n\nAvailable in print:\n\n04 July 2019\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780190938055\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n\n### Contents\n\n[Input]\n\n  1. Expand Front Matter\n\n  2. Introduction: “One of the Things You Learn as President Is That You’re Always Dealing with Probabilities”\n\n  3. Collapse 1 Pathologies of Probability Assessment\n\n     * Expand “Words of Estimative Probability”\n\n       * Words of Estimative Probability Outside Intelligence Studies Words of Estimative Probability Outside Intelligence Studies\n\n     * Expand Relative Probability and Conditioning\n\n     * Expand The Probability of Success in Vietnam, 1961–1965\n\n       * Expanding the War, 1964–1965\n\n       * Measuring Tactical Progress without Assessing Strategic Prospects Measuring Tactical Progress without Assessing Strategic Prospects\n\n     * Patterns of Probability Assessment across Foreign Relations Documents Patterns of Probability Assessment across Foreign Relations Documents\n\n     * Beyond “Words of Estimative Probability”\n\n  4. 2 Subjective Probability and International Politics\n\n  5. 3 The Value of Precision in Probability Assessment\n\n  6. 4 Dispelling Illusions of Rigor\n\n  7. 5 The Politics of Uncertainty and Blame\n\n  8. 6 Analysis and Decision\n\n  9. 7 Placing Probability Front and Center in Foreign Policy Discourse\n\n  10. Expand End Matter\n\n[Input]\n\n  * < Previous chapter\n  * Next chapter >\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\nChapter\n\n# 1 Pathologies of Probability Assessment\n\nGet access\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nFind on\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.003.0002\n\nPages\n\n17–50\n\n  * Published:\n\n17 April 2019\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis chapter describes how foreign policy analysts often avoid assessing uncertainty in a manner that supports sound decision making. This aversion to probabilistic reasoning can take three forms: imprecise judgments that do not establish clear meaning; judgments of relative probability, which frame assessments of uncertainty against unspecified baselines; and conditioning, a practice that identifies the assumptions that must hold for a statement to be true but says nothing about the probability that a conclusion is actually correct. The chapter shows how official doctrine for intelligence analysis and military planning encourages foreign policy analysts to assess uncertainty in these problematic ways. It then shows how these problematic methods of assessing uncertainty shaped the highest levels of U.S. decision making during the Vietnam War, focusing on how senior leaders crafted military strategies without carefully assessing the chances that they would succeed.\n\nKeywords: international relations, national security, decision making, uncertainty, risk assessment, probability, forecasting, intelligence analysis, military planning, Vietnam War\n\nSubject\n\nInternational Relations\n\nCollection: Oxford Scholarship Online\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics. Jeffrey A. Friedman, Oxford University Press (2019). © Jeffrey A. Friedman. DOI: 10.1093/oso/9780190938024.003.0002--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDedication | War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/34986/chapter-abstract/298660600)\nciteturn46search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International PoliticsJeffrey A.Friedman ... 17 April 2019\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.001.0001\n\nPublished:\n\n17 April 2019\n\nAvailable in print:\n\n04 July 2019\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780190938055\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780190938024\n\nFront Matter\n\n# Dedication\n\nGet access\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nFind on\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.002.0005\n\nPages\n\nv–vi\n\n  * Published:\n\n17 April 2019\n\n## Extract\n\nFor my parents\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAcknowledgments | War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/34986/chapter-abstract/298660605)\nciteturn46search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International PoliticsJeffrey A.Friedman ... 17 April 2019 ... Stephen Walt, Monica Toft, and Robert Bates were my dissertation advisers at Harvard.In addition to providing careful feedback on my research over many years, each of them has served as a role model for producing scholarship that is interesting, rigorous, and policy-relevant.\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.001.0001\n\nPublished:\n\n17 April 2019\n\nAvailable in print:\n\n04 July 2019\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780190938055\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780190938024\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\n# Acknowledgments\n\nGet access\n\n  * Published:\n\n17 April 2019\n\n## Extract\n\nI am delighted to have so many people to thank for their input, guidance, and support.\n\nStephen Walt, Monica Toft, and Robert Bates were my dissertation advisers at Harvard. In addition to providing careful feedback on my research over many years, each of them has served as a role model for producing scholarship that is interesting, rigorous, and policy-relevant. I am also especially grateful for the mentorship of Richard Zeckhauser, who inspired my interest in decision analysis, and Stephen Biddle, who played a crucial role in launching my academic career.\n\nDartmouth has provided an ideal environment in which to study international politics. It has been wonderful to be surrounded by so many outstanding faculty who work in this area, including Dan Benjamin, Steve Brooks, Udi Greenberg, Brian Greenhill, Jenny Lind, Mike Mastanduno, Ed Miller, Jennie Miller, Nick Miller, Katy Powers, Daryl Press, Ben Valentino, and Bill Wohlforth. I was lucky to arrive at Dartmouth along with a cohort of brilliant, supportive junior scholars, especially David Cottrell, Derek Epp, Jeremy Ferwerda, Herschel Nachlis, Paul Novosad, Julie Rose, Na’ama Shenhav, Sean Westwood, and Thomas Youle. Dartmouth’s undergraduates have also served as a valuable sounding board for many of the ideas presented in this book. It has been a pleasure to work with them and to learn from their insights.\n\nSubject\n\nInternational Relations\n\nCollection: Oxford Scholarship Online--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnalysis and Decision | War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/34986/chapter-abstract/298661718)\nciteturn46search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; War and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International PoliticsJeffrey A. ... Friedman, Oxford University Press (2019).\n\nWar and Chance: Assessing Uncertainty in International Politics\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.001.0001\n\nPublished:\n\n17 April 2019\n\nAvailable in print:\n\n04 July 2019\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780190938055\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780190938024\n\nChapter\n\n# 6 Analysis and Decision\n\nGet access\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nJeffrey A. Friedman\n\nFind on\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780190938024.003.0007\n\nPages\n\n161–186\n\n  * Published:\n\n17 April 2019\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis chapter explains how decision makers can incorporate assessments of uncertainty into high-stakes foreign policy choices. It begins by describing a simple analytic tool called break-even analysis, with which leaders can use explicit probability assessments as a point of leverage for determining whether or not a risky decision is worthwhile. The chapter then explains how transparent probabilistic reasoning is especially important for assessing strategic progress. In some cases, it can actually be impossible to make rigorous judgments about the extent to which foreign policies are making acceptable progress without assessing subjective probabilities in detail. This argument departs from a large body of existing scholarship on learning in international politics that assumes leaders can use a straightforward logic of trial and error to determine how they should update their strategic perceptions over time. The chapter provides examples of these dynamics drawn from the U.S. occupation of Iraq.\n\nKeywords: international relations, national security, decision making, uncertainty, military 